    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 mt-2">Arm and wrist acupressure covers points with surprising reach: PC6 at the wrist for nausea and motion sickness, LI4 at the thumb web for headaches, and several forearm points for localized arm and wrist pain.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="mb-10 flex justify-center"><svg viewBox="0 0 60 160" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-16 h-40"><path d="M20 10 Q15 40 10 70 Q8 100 10 130 Q12 145 30 145 Q48 145 50 130 Q52 100 50 70 Q45 40 40 10 Z" fill="rgba(45,106,79,0.06)" stroke="#2d6a4f" stroke-width="1.5"></path><circle cx="30" cy="30" r="2" fill="#2d6a4f" opacity="0.5"></circle><circle cx="30" cy="70" r="2" fill="#2d6a4f" opacity="0.5"></circle><circle cx="30" cy="100" r="2" fill="#2d6a4f" opacity="0.5"></circle><circle cx="30" cy="130" r="2" fill="#2d6a4f" opacity="0.5"></circle><text x="30" y="158" font-family="system-ui" font-size="10" fill="#2d6a4f" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="600">Arm</text></svg></div>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Why Arm and Wrist Acupressure Points Matter</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">The arms and wrists carry some of the most versatile points in the entire system — locations that treat complaints far beyond the limb itself. LI4 near the thumb web is a classic target for headaches and toothaches, while PC6 at the wrist is traditionally used for nausea, motion sickness, and anxiety, and LU7 keeps the pattern pointing at neck and head complaints. For problems in the limb itself, such as repetitive strain, tennis-elbow discomfort, wrist pain, and general fatigue, the same band of forearm points provides local relief. These locations pair naturally with our guides to acupressure for nausea, headaches, and neck pain.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Classically, PC6 sits on the Pericardium meridian, LI4 and LI10 on the Large Intestine meridian, and LU7 on the Lung meridian — pathways that link the arms to the head, chest, and digestion. Physically, the wrist and forearm pack dense nerve structures close to the surface, and sustained pressure in these channels is traditionally linked to easing nausea signals and relaxing tense forearm muscles. PC6 has been among the most researched acupressure points since the nausea studies of the 1980s, which is why it appears so often in clinical and self-care contexts.</p>
  </section>

