  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">How to Stimulate Arm and Wrist Points</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">These points are best reached with the pad of your thumb on the opposite hand. Rest the arm on a stable surface, press at a 90-degree angle, and deepen the pressure gradually over 10–15 seconds until you feel a deep, dull ache (deqi). Hold for 30–60 seconds, then repeat on the other arm. Keep the wrist relaxed and the pressure steady rather than squeezing. LI4 should not be pressed during pregnancy, and if any point causes sharp pain or tingling, ease off immediately.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Where is PC6 for nausea?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">PC6 (Neiguan) sits on the inner forearm, about three finger-widths below the wrist crease, in the gap between the two long tendons that appear when your palm faces up. The point lies roughly in the middle of that gap. Press downward with your thumb and you should feel a deep, heavy sensation rather than a sharp one. This is the location targeted by most acupressure wristbands for motion sickness and nausea.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can wrist acupressure really stop motion sickness?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">No single technique can guarantee stopping motion sickness, and acupressure is not a substitute for medical travel remedies. That said, PC6 is one of the most-studied points in modern research, with a long tradition of nausea and vomiting studies that began in the 1980s. Many people find that pressing the point firmly at the earliest sign of queasiness, for 30–60 seconds on both wrists, eases the sensation. It is a low-cost, low-risk tool worth trying alongside fresh air and a fixed visual horizon.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long should I press these points?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">For the wrist and forearm, hold each point for 30–60 seconds on the go, or up to 1–2 minutes for deeper forearm points such as LI10. Press both arms to keep both sides balanced, increase pressure slowly over 10–15 seconds, and breathe evenly until you feel the deep, dull deqi sensation. Repeat as needed through the day, especially before travel or long sessions at a keyboard.</div>
    </details>
  </section>

  <div class="mt-10 text-center">
    <a href="/atlas/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-stone-500 hover:text-forest-600 dark:text-stone-400 dark:hover:text-forest-300">Back to Body Atlas</a>
